\section{It is a different concept from group exemption, and must stay one}{

A §501(h) affiliated group is defined by interlocking governance under §4911 -
one organisation bound by another's decisions on legislative issues, or enough
board overlap to control them. That is a \strong{control test}, not the group
exemption process, so these edges must never enter the \code{group_id} components
(\code{GROUP-STRUCTURE-FINDINGS.md} §2).

They land as \code{related_entity} (§4a), the same value Schedule R Part II uses,
and the measured overlap is the argument: \strong{834 of 886 edges (94\%) already
exist as a Schedule R Part II edge}. A looser coordination tie - a
\code{coalition} in the §4a sense - would not overlap Schedule R that heavily.
The word "affiliated" in the statute names a control relationship, not a
federated chapter structure.
}

\section{It is reciprocal, which is why the self-loops matter}{

Every electing member reports the \emph{whole} group, so a five-member group
produces five rosters that each name all five. In TY2013, \strong{78 of 123 filers
list their own EIN}, and 652 rows collapse to 339 distinct member EINs.
Self-references are dropped; the rest are stored in the disclosed direction
(filer -> member) like every other relation, and a consumer wanting the
undirected view reads the pair both ways.
}

\section{It is nearly redundant, and is loaded anyway for the provenance}{

Measured on TY2013, \strong{394 of 430 undirected pairs (91.6\%) already exist as
Schedule R Part II edges}. This source adds tens of pairs, not thousands.

It earns its place on the \code{evidence} tag rather than the coverage: an edge
corroborated by both a Schedule R disclosure and a §501(h) affiliated-group
filing is a stronger claim than either alone, and only this ingest can say so.
Loading it to inflate the row count would be the wrong reason.
}

